<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
    android:background="@drawable/shape_rounded_item_corner"
    android:clipToPadding="false"
    android:orientation="vertical">

    <LinearLayout
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:clipToPadding="false"
        android:gravity="center"
        android:paddingHorizontal="16dp"
        android:translationZ="1dp">

        <View
            android:id="@+id/trackCoverPlaceHolder"
            android:layout_width="@dimen/collapsed_cover_size"
            android:layout_height="match_parent"
            android:layout_marginEnd="12dp"
            android:visibility="gone" />

        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="0dp"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_weight="1"
            android:orientation="vertical">

            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/trackTitle"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:ellipsize="marquee"
                android:gravity="bottom"
                android:maxLines="1"
                android:minHeight="24dp"
                android:textSize="20sp"
                android:textStyle="bold"
                tools:text="Bruh" />

            <TextView
                android:id="@+id/trackArtist"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:ellipsize="end"
                android:maxLines="2"
                android:paddingBottom="20dp"
                android:textColorLink="@color/button_player"
                android:textSize="16sp"
                tools:text="@tools:sample/lorem/random" />
        </LinearLayout>

        <com.google.android.material.checkbox.MaterialCheckBox
            android:id="@+id/trackHeart"
            android:layout_width="64dp"
            android:layout_height="64dp"
            android:background="@drawable/ripple_60dp"
            android:button="@drawable/anim_heart_36dp"
            android:contentDescription="@string/like"
            app:buttonTint="@color/button_player_heart" />

        <!-- Overflow, moved down from the toolbar's menu (more_menu / menu_more) so it sits beside the
             heart rather than in the top right, which the extension icon now occupies. It is a plain
             view with a click listener, NOT a menu item, so PlayerToolbarStyle no longer sets `menu`.
             Whichever control is LAST owns the end margin. trackHeart is gone entirely when the
             extension has no LikeClient (isVisible is gated on isTrackClient in PlayerFragment), so
             this must not depend on the heart being present - it is a sibling of the row, not of
             the heart.

             [CORRECTED 2026-09-14] THIS COMMENT SAID "the row has 24dp horizontal padding". IT IS
             16dp, AND THAT DISCREPANCY IS THE WHOLE STORY OF A REGRESSION. The -20dp end margin was
             calibrated against 24dp; `git log -S` puts the 24dp -> 16dp change in 554164ce ("Fix the
             player's icon sizes and spacing"), while the -20dp predates it (ea3c5f7c / b4b63389, the
             original player UI). Nobody revisited the margin, so the glyph moved 8dp closer to the
             screen edge - from 14dp to 6dp - and the row started reading as cramped with nothing
             visibly broken. The stale number is kept above rather than deleted because it is the
             evidence: a margin whose stated basis no longer matches the file is the thing to check.

             THE ARITHMETIC, so the next person does not re-derive it. Distance from the row's right
             edge to the glyph is rowPaddingRight + marginEnd + buttonPaddingRight. The button's
             10dp padding is Material's own m3_btn_icon_only_default_padding (Widget.Material3
             .Button.IconButton, read from the 1.14.0 AAR) and is NOT overridden here; 10 + 28 + 10 =
             48 exactly, so the glyph position is forced by arithmetic and does not depend on whether
             MaterialButton centres or start-aligns an icon with no text.
                 -20dp gave 16 - 20 + 10 = 6dp
                 -10dp gives 16 - 10 + 10 = 16dp   <- matches PlayerTrackCoverContainer's 16dp
                                                      marginEnd (the album art's right edge) and the
                                                      row's own 16dp paddingLeft (the title's left
                                                      edge). That grid is the target; the transport
                                                      row below sits at 22dp and is the outlier.

             AND -20dp PUT 4dp OF THIS BUTTON OUTSIDE ITS PARENT. The view's right edge landed 4dp
             past the row's right edge, so the row (clipChildren defaults true) clipped it and ~4dp
             of the 48dp touch target lay outside the row, where touches are not dispatched. It
             LOOKED fine only because Widget.Material3.Button.IconButton also sets android:insetLeft
             /insetRight = m3_btn_inset = 4dp, which shrinks the drawn background by exactly the
             amount being clipped - so the ripple stopped precisely at the row edge. THAT WAS A
             COINCIDENCE OF TWO UNRELATED 4dp VALUES, NOT A DESIGN. -10dp removes the overhang
             entirely. If anyone re-introduces a margin more negative than -16dp here, the button
             leaves the parent again and the coincidence will not necessarily cover it. -->
        <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
            android:id="@+id/trackMore"
            style="@style/Widget.Material3.Button.IconButton"
            android:layout_width="48dp"
            android:layout_height="48dp"
            android:layout_gravity="center_vertical"
            android:layout_marginEnd="-10dp"
            android:contentDescription="@string/more"
            android:insetTop="0dp"
            android:insetBottom="0dp"
            app:icon="@drawable/ic_more_vert"
            app:iconGravity="textStart"
            app:iconPadding="0dp"
            app:iconSize="28dp"
            app:iconTint="@color/button_player" />

    </LinearLayout>

    <!-- This negative margin pulls the seek assembly UP over the title/artist row. It has to be negative
         at all because the Slider is far taller than it looks: BaseSlider.onMeasure sizes to
         max(minWidgetThickness, max(trackHeight, thumbHeight) + paddingTop + paddingBottom) = 44 + 76 =
         120dp here, nearly all of it empty space around a 4dp track. Without the pull, the region below the
         artist reads as a large void.
         Relaxed from -36dp to -20dp in the spacing pass: at -36dp the 44dp thumb reached into the artist
         line, which was the "middle is packed" symptom. The 16dp handed back is the single largest
         contributor to the crowding between artist, seek bar and timestamps.
         This GROWS the block by 16dp, and that is intended. Growth is safe and self correcting:
         PlayerFragment measures this layout into uiViewModel.playerControlsHeight, and
         PlayerTrackAdapter:157 writes that onto item_player_track.xml's player_controls_placeholder, so the
         page's cover position and the collapse morph target (toMoveY = placeholder.top - cover.top) both
         follow it. The 107dp in that placeholder is a design time value only.
         Growth also SHRINKS the end gaps rather than the reverse: with the cover chain packed (see
         PlayerTrackCoverContainer) every dp added here comes out of the leftover pooled at the two ends,
         not out of the cover to title gap, which is now fixed by the cover's own marginBottom. -->
    <FrameLayout
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="-20dp"
        android:clipToPadding="false"
        android:paddingHorizontal="12dp"
        android:translationZ="-1dp">

        <FrameLayout
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content">

            <!-- Despite the id, this now draws ONLY the inactive rail: its buffer indicator is
                 deliberately transparent. DeterminateDrawable.setIndicatorFraction() assigns endFraction
                 and nothing ever assigns startFraction, so it keeps its 0f default — a LinearProgressIndicator
                 can only ever fill from the LEFT EDGE to `progress`. With buffered >= position that put a
                 solid accent line under the entire played portion, showing through the wave wherever the
                 4dp band swung clear of the centre line: the "wave oscillating around a straight line".
                 There is no way to draw only the useful playhead->buffered segment from one indicator
                 (ProgressBar.min rescales the fraction, it does not offset the start).
                 fillTrack() is called with (0f, 1f) unconditionally, so the rail stays continuous across
                 the full width with the indicator transparent — no gap where it used to be.
                 progress is still wired in PlayerFragment's progress observer so this is a two-line
                 reversal: restore ?attr/colorTertiary here AND the setIndicatorColor call in
                 PlayerFragment's playerColors block, which otherwise overrides this at runtime.
                 KNOWN COST: no buffer display on the expanded player. Real on a slow connection.
                 tv_buffer_bar carries the same change; keep the two in step. -->
            <!-- RAIL COLOUR AND ALPHA ARE LOAD-BEARING TOGETHER - see PlayerFragment's seekWaveBar
                 block for the full rationale.
                 amoled_fg, NOT ?attr/colorOnSurface: the wave now uses amoled_fg too, and ONE SOURCE for
                 both halves is the point - colorOnSurface is wallpaper-derived under DynamicColors on
                 Android 12+, so the two halves could drift apart per device.
                 THE ALPHA NOW CARRIES THE PLAYED/UNPLAYED DISTINCTION. While the wave was artwork-tinted,
                 hue separated the halves and the 2dp-vs-4dp thickness step was a secondary cue. With both
                 halves the same hue, thickness alone provides NO CONTRAST AT THE BOUNDARY - solid-versus-0.3
                 is what separates them. Do not raise this back toward the wave's opacity, and do not equalise
                 the thicknesses. -->
            <com.google.android.material.progressindicator.LinearProgressIndicator
                android:id="@+id/bufferBar"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_gravity="center"
                android:layout_marginHorizontal="12dp"
                android:alpha="0.3"
                android:progress="40"
                app:indicatorColor="@android:color/transparent"
                app:indicatorTrackGapSize="0dp"
                app:trackColor="@color/amoled_fg"
                app:trackThickness="2dp" />

            <!-- Wavy POSITION track. Ordered between bufferBar and seekBar deliberately: later children
                 draw on top, so the wave covers the buffer's inactive track but the Slider's thumb still
                 draws over the wave. Moving this after seekBar would paint the wave over the thumb.
                 The Slider keeps ownership of touch, value and seeking; this draws the active line only,
                 which is why seekBar's trackColorActive is transparent (and why PlayerFragment's
                 playerColors block tints THIS view rather than the Slider's track).
                 layout_height is pinned rather than wrap_content. LinearDrawingDelegate.getPreferredHeight()
                 is `trackThickness + waveAmplitude * 2` (read from the 1.14.0 artifact), so the wave's real
                 extent here is 4 + 20 = 24dp. THE PIN MUST BE RAISED WITH THE AMPLITUDE — at 28dp it clears
                 24dp with slack, but an amplitude bump alone would start clipping the crests silently
                 (symptom: a trapezoidal, flat-topped wave that does not get taller when you raise
                 waveAmplitude). Keep the slack at 4dp: adjustCanvas offsets the drawing DOWN by
                 max(0, (boundsHeight - preferredHeight) / 2), so changing the gap moves the wave off the
                 rail's centre line.
                 bufferBar above is deliberately THINNER than this (2dp vs 4dp): the wave and the rail never
                 coexist spatially — left of the thumb the wave replaces the rail, right of it only the rail
                 shows — so a thicker active over a thinner inactive reads as hierarchy, not as overhang.
                 Matching them made the played and unplayed halves carry equal visual weight.
                 The Slider's thumb is a 4dp x 44dp BAR, not a circle: both are Material's own
                 m3_comp_slider_active_handle_width/_height.
                 Material's 44:16 thumb-to-track ratio (2.75:1) would want ~66dp against this 24dp band, and
                 that is DELIBERATELY not taken: thumbHeight feeds widgetThickness in BaseSlider.onMeasure,
                 which sizes this Slider, which propagates to uiViewModel.playerControlsHeight and the
                 collapse morph — 20->28->44 has already spent that budget twice and 66dp compounds it.
                 44dp still clears the 24dp band by 10dp each side, so the thumb reads as standing over the
                 crests rather than being swallowed, which is the property that actually matters.
                 Width stays 4dp: thumbWidth is horizontal and does not feed widgetThickness, so it can be
                 widened at any time at zero geometric risk; thumbHeight cannot.
                 thumbHeight feeds widgetThickness in BaseSlider.onMeasure, which sizes this Slider, which
                 propagates to uiViewModel.playerControlsHeight and the collapse morph. Whether it actually
                 moves depends on whether minTouchTargetSize=90dp already dominates. The test is free: drag
                 the player down and watch the cover morph — if it morphs correctly, the height did not move.
                 importantForAccessibility=no: this duplicates the value the Slider already exposes through
                 contentDescription, and TalkBack should not announce a second progress bar for it. -->
            <com.google.android.material.progressindicator.LinearProgressIndicator
                android:id="@+id/seekWaveBar"
                style="@style/EchoLinearProgressIndicator.Wavy"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="28dp"
                android:layout_gravity="center"
                android:layout_marginHorizontal="12dp"
                android:importantForAccessibility="no"
                app:indicatorColor="?attr/colorTertiary"
                app:waveAmplitude="10dp"
                app:wavelength="40dp"
                app:trackThickness="4dp" />

            <!-- Thumb is a 4dp x 20dp BAR (Material's own default handle width), not a circle. TV
                 deliberately keeps a 24dp circle — see the note at fragment_player_tv.xml's tv_seek_bar
                 for why, and do not unify the two. -->
            <com.google.android.material.slider.Slider
                android:id="@+id/seekBar"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginStart="-3dp"
                android:layout_marginEnd="-3dp"
                android:contentDescription="@string/seekbar"
                android:paddingVertical="38dp"
                android:value="0.5"
                app:labelBehavior="gone"
                app:minTouchTargetSize="90dp"
                app:thumbColor="?attr/colorTertiary"
                app:thumbHeight="44dp"
                app:thumbWidth="4dp"
                app:trackColorActive="@android:color/transparent"
                app:trackColorInactive="@android:color/transparent"
                app:trackHeight="4dp"
                app:trackStopIndicatorSize="0dp" />
        </FrameLayout>

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/trackCurrentTime"
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_gravity="bottom"
            android:layout_marginTop="24dp"
            android:elevation="1dp"
            android:maxLines="1"
            android:padding="12dp"
            tools:text="00:00" />

        <!-- The metadata pill: same line as the timestamps, elapsed left / pill centred / total right.
             Measured before sizing rather than estimated: "MPEG - 44100 Hz - 2ch - 320 kbps" is 185dp in
             Roboto at 12sp, and the gap between the timestamps is ~215dp even for 1:23:45 durations
             (387dp row minus two 70dp timestamps minus breathing room), so the typical string does NOT
             ellipsize. At 16sp it would be 243dp and would clip on every track - which is why the text is
             smaller here than in the full-width label it replaces. The pill is glanceable, not read.
             maxWidth is ONE dimen with NO values-land override: landscape is SHORT, not narrow (the
             controls column is a weighted half of ~914dp, comparable to portrait's 411dp), so an override
             would add the same drift risk flagged on collapsed_end_controls_width for no gain.
             Ellipsis is the right outcome when it does happen - merged servers contribute one source
             title each, video streams add a whole segment - because the full string is one tap away in
             QualitySelectionBottomSheet. minHeight is what keeps that tap target a target at all,
             while the visual pill stays short via the background's vertical inset.
             [CORRECTED 2026-10-16] THIS SENTENCE SAID "minHeight keeps that tap target at 48dp".
             IT IS 40dp, AND HAS BEEN SINCE THE AUGUST PASS - see the minHeight paragraph directly
             below, which explains at length why 48dp was given up (a bottom-gravity 48dp box
             occupies 80-120dp of the 120dp seek FrameLayout and touches the 44dp thumb). The two
             halves of this comment contradicted each other, with the wrong number stated first.
             The decision stands; only the sentence was wrong.
             minHeight is 40dp, NOT the usual 48dp: the seek FrameLayout is exactly widgetThickness tall
             (BaseSlider sizes to max(minWidgetThickness, max(trackThickness, thumbHeight) + paddingTop +
             paddingBottom) = 44 + 76 = 120dp here), the wave band is centred so it occupies 48-72dp, and a
             48dp pill at bottom gravity occupies 72-120dp - touching it exactly. The timestamps beside it
             are ~40dp and clear the wave by 8dp, so the pill was simply taller than its neighbours.
             Buying that clearance any other way costs block height: paddingVertical is additive to
             widgetThickness, and only HALF of any increase becomes clearance (the wave centre moves down
             by half, the pill by all of it), so 8dp of gap would cost 16dp of playerControlsHeight.
             40dp is defensible here because this is a SECONDARY affordance - the same QualitySelection
             sheet is reachable from the quality controls - and 48dp is a guideline for primary targets. -->
        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/trackSubtitle"
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_gravity="center|bottom"
            android:background="@drawable/shape_pill"
            android:elevation="1dp"
            android:ellipsize="end"
            android:gravity="center"
            android:maxLines="1"
            android:maxWidth="@dimen/player_pill_max_width"
            android:minHeight="40dp"
            android:paddingHorizontal="10dp"
            android:textColor="@color/amoled_fg"
            android:textSize="12sp"
            tools:text="MPEG - 44100 Hz - 2ch - 320 kbps" />

        <TextView
            android:id="@+id/trackTotalTime"
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:layout_gravity="end|bottom"
            android:alpha="0.66"
            android:elevation="1dp"
            android:maxLines="1"
            android:padding="12dp"
            tools:text="00:00" />
    </FrameLayout>

    <androidx.constraintlayout.widget.ConstraintLayout
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:layout_marginTop="8dp"
        android:paddingHorizontal="4dp">

        <!-- SHUFFLE LEFT, REPEAT RIGHT (2026-10-16). These two were the other way round and this
             player was the only surface in the app that had them inverted: fragment_player_tv.xml's
             transport row is already "shuffle | prev | play/pause | next | repeat", and the Android
             Auto / notification custom layout is already built shuffle-first in Kotlin
             (PlayerCallback.onConnect's seed and PlayerEventListener.updateCustomLayout both list
             getShuffleButton before getRepeatButton). So this is the phone catching up with its own
             app, not a new convention - and it is layout-only, since every listener binds by id.
             THE SWAP IS FOUR ATTRIBUTES, NOT TWO: the bias here and on trackRepeat, PLUS the chain
             references on trackPrevious and trackNext. Those two are constrained BETWEEN the outer
             buttons and the centre, so leaving their old targets in place collapses the row.
             backgroundTint here is only the pre-colour default; PlayerFragment's playerColors
             observer replaces it with a state list (checked -> colorPrimary, else 12% of
             PlayerColors.onBackground). See bg_player_transport_circle.xml.
             anim_shuffle_plain, NOT anim_shuffle: the boxed ON glyph would nest inside the filled
             circle, and anim_shuffle is shared with the TV player. -->
        <com.google.android.material.checkbox.MaterialCheckBox
            android:id="@+id/trackShuffle"
            android:layout_width="64dp"
            android:layout_height="64dp"
            android:background="@drawable/bg_player_transport_circle"
            android:backgroundTint="@color/player_transport_circle_off"
            android:button="@drawable/anim_shuffle_plain"
            android:contentDescription="@string/shuffle"
            android:elevation="2dp"
            android:outlineProvider="none"
            app:buttonTint="@color/button_player"
            app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
            app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
            app:layout_constraintHorizontal_bias="0.0"
            app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
            app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent" />

        <!-- ⚠️ PINNED TO 64dp, WHICH IT HAD TO BE TO CARRY A CIRCLE. It was
             wrap_content, and a tonal background needs a known box or the circle tracks the glyph's
             measured width. 64dp matches trackShuffle and trackRepeat, so all four satellites draw
             the same 48dp circle from bg_player_transport_circle.xml's single 8dp inset.
             ROW HEIGHT IS UNCHANGED, which is the constraint that matters here: this
             ConstraintLayout is wrap_content and playPauseContainer is 72dp, so 64dp stays under
             the tallest child and nothing propagates to uiViewModel.playerControlsHeight,
             player_controls_placeholder, or the collapse morph's toMoveY.
             The glyph stays 40dp with ~21dp of ink (ic_skip_previous_40dp's paths span 9.2-30.9 of
             a 40 viewport), so it sits comfortably inside the 48dp circle; iconSize was left alone
             deliberately rather than retuned by eye. -->
        <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
            android:id="@+id/trackPrevious"
            style="?attr/materialIconButtonStyle"
            android:layout_width="64dp"
            android:layout_height="64dp"
            android:background="@drawable/bg_player_transport_circle"
            android:backgroundTint="@color/player_transport_circle_off"
            android:contentDescription="@string/previous"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:outlineProvider="none"
            app:elevation="1dp"
            app:icon="@drawable/ic_skip_previous_40dp"
            app:iconSize="40dp"
            app:iconTint="@color/button_player"
            app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
            app:layout_constraintEnd_toStartOf="@+id/playPauseContainer"
            app:layout_constraintStart_toEndOf="@id/trackShuffle"
            app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent" />

        <FrameLayout
            android:id="@+id/playPauseContainer"
            android:layout_width="72dp"
            android:layout_height="72dp"
            app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
            app:layout_constraintEnd_toEndOf="parent"
            app:layout_constraintStart_toStartOf="parent"
            app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent">

            <com.google.android.material.progressindicator.CircularProgressIndicator
                android:id="@+id/playingIndicator"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="match_parent"
                android:clickable="false"
                android:indeterminate="true"
                app:indicatorSize="64dp" />

            <!-- ⚠⚠ THE FOCAL CONTROL: A 56dp FILLED DISC IN AN UNCHANGED 72dp BOX.
                 The fill, the inset geometry and the playingIndicator clearance are all argued at
                 bg_player_play_pause.xml; the two things that live here are the tint and the glyph.
                 buttonTint is ?attr/colorOnPrimary - the ONE transport glyph that is not
                 PlayerColors.onBackground, because it is the only one drawn on top of an opaque
                 fill rather than on the artwork. It is also the only one PlayerFragment's
                 playerColors observer leaves alone, deliberately: theme pairing already guarantees
                 its contrast against the disc, so a Palette colour here could only break it.
                 ⚠️ THE GLYPH STAYS anim_play_pause_48dp, AND 48dp IS THE BOX, NOT THE INK.
                 Measured before deciding: ic_play_48dp's path spans 320-720 x 252-728 of a 960
                 viewport and ic_pause_48dp's spans 230-730 x 200-760, so the drawn glyph is about
                 25-28dp - already the "~32dp" the design asked for, with 14dp of clear disc around
                 it. Forcing a 32dp INTRINSIC would have shrunk the ink to ~19dp and cost four new
                 duplicated vectors (the animated-selector's two states plus two transitions), so
                 the smaller number would have been both more work and visually worse. If this is
                 ever revisited, change the ink, not the box. -->
            <com.google.android.material.checkbox.MaterialCheckBox
                android:id="@+id/trackPlayPause"
                android:layout_width="72dp"
                android:layout_height="72dp"
                android:background="@drawable/bg_player_play_pause"
                android:button="@drawable/anim_play_pause_48dp"
                android:checked="true"
                android:contentDescription="@string/play"
                app:buttonTint="?attr/colorOnPrimary"
                tools:ignore="DuplicateSpeakableTextCheck" />
        </FrameLayout>

        <!-- Pinned to 64dp for the same reason as trackPrevious; see the note there.
             constraintEnd_toStartOf is now trackRepeat, and it keeps the "@+id" form because
             trackRepeat is declared AFTER this view in the file - a forward reference without the
             plus does not resolve. trackPrevious's new target, trackShuffle, is declared earlier,
             so that one is a plain "@id". -->
        <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
            android:id="@+id/trackNext"
            style="?attr/materialIconButtonStyle"
            android:layout_width="64dp"
            android:layout_height="64dp"
            android:background="@drawable/bg_player_transport_circle"
            android:backgroundTint="@color/player_transport_circle_off"
            android:contentDescription="@string/next"
            android:enabled="false"
            android:outlineProvider="none"
            app:elevation="1dp"
            app:icon="@drawable/ic_skip_next_40dp"
            app:iconSize="40dp"
            app:iconTint="@color/button_player"
            app:layout_constraintBottom_toBottomOf="parent"
            app:layout_constraintEnd_toStartOf="@+id/trackRepeat"
            app:layout_constraintStart_toEndOf="@id/playPauseContainer"
            app:layout_constraintTop_toTopOf="parent" />

        <!-- trackRepeat and trackShuffle are BOTH 64dp boxes but size their glyphs by different
             mechanisms, which is why they looked mismatched: trackShuffle is a MaterialCheckBox whose
             glyph comes from the button drawable's INTRINSIC size (anim_shuffle_plain -> ic_shuffle_40dp
             / ic_shuffle_on_plain_40dp, both 28dp - it was anim_shuffle until 2026-10-16),
             while this is a MaterialButton sizing its icon explicitly via app:iconSize. The 12dp padding
             was never the cause - iconSize was 32dp against shuffle's 28dp. Keep these two numbers equal;
             changing padding will not fix a glyph-size mismatch on a MaterialButton. -->
        <!-- Moved to the END (bias 1.0) - see the swap note at trackShuffle.
             ⚠⚠ REPEAT'S ON-STATE IS CARRIED TWICE, AND IT HAS TO BE, BECAUSE IT HAS
             THREE STATES AND A FILL ONLY HAS TWO. The circle separates OFF from on; the glyph
             separates all three, and that distinction is already in the drawables rather than
             something added here: ic_repeat_off_40dp is ic_repeat_40dp plus a visible diagonal
             slash, and ic_repeat_one_40dp is the same glyph plus a "1" (its slash is present at
             strokeAlpha=0 purely so ic_repeat_to_repeat_one_40dp can morph it). So: slash = OFF,
             plain = ALL, "1" = ONE, and the fill answers only "is repeat doing anything".
             ⚠️ THE FILL IS DRIVEN BY android:state_activated, NOT state_checked.
             This is a MaterialButton, not a checkbox - repeat mode is not a view state - so
             PlayerFragment's changeRepeatDrawable sets isActivated = (mode != REPEAT_MODE_OFF) and
             the colour lives entirely in the playerColors observer's state lists. Do not convert
             this to a checkbox to "simplify" it: a two-state widget cannot represent three modes,
             which is why it is a button in the first place.
             iconTint stays @color/amoled_fg as the pre-colour default only; the observer replaces
             it with a PlayerColors.onBackground state list. -->
        <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
            android:id="@+id/trackRepeat"
            style="?attr/materialIconButtonStyle"
            android:layout_width="64dp"
